  \section{Summary}\label{summary}
  
  Reflectance describes how a surface reflects light at different
  wavelengths. SpectralBridge is Python research software that helps teams
  prepare and check reflectance products from NEON airborne sensors,
  drones, and Landsat. It retains named intermediate rasters, extracted
  values, quality checks, and provenance so users can inspect how each
  product was made. Separate routes preserve sensor-specific operations,
  while collection tools summarize selected flightlines and sites. The
  package organizes established corrections rather than proposing a new
  algorithm. Tests and team research examples demonstrate software
  contracts and limitations, not agreement among sensors.
  
  \section{Statement of need}\label{statement-of-need}
  
  Band names invite direct comparison, but spectral response, footprint,
  viewing direction, and illumination help define the reflectance recorded
  by each instrument. A drone may isolate sunlit foliage; NEON records a
  dense spectrum; Landsat mixes canopy, gaps, soil, and shadow across
  broader pixels (\citeproc{ref-paper_kampe2010}{Kampe et al., 2010};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_nationalecologicalobservatorynetwork2026}{National
  Ecological Observatory Network, 2026};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_woodcock1987}{Woodcock \& Strahler, 1987}). Matching
  labels therefore do not make measurements equivalent, and these
  differences can change an ecological interpretation
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_huang2002}{Huang et al., 2002};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_nicodemus1977}{Nicodemus et al., 1977};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_roy2016}{Roy et al., 2016};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_schaepmanstrub2006}{Schaepman-Strub et al., 2006};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_teillet1997}{Teillet, 1997}). The intended users are
  remote-sensing researchers and developers who need to construct
  sensor-oriented products, inspect intermediate states, verify outputs,
  or summarize flightlines. They remain responsible for temporal matching,
  co-registration, and common spatial support. Independent validation also
  requires characterized reference measurements and uncertainty analysis;
  process completion alone cannot establish it
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_origo2020}{Origo et al., 2020}). SpectralBridge
  preserves the sequence from source through configured transformations to
  extracted values, helping users locate where an unexpected pattern first
  appears. It does not diagnose discrepancies or choose a valid
  spatial/temporal design. \autoref{fig:paper1} illustrates the separate
  roles of spatial support and spectral response.
  
  \begin{figure}
  \centering
  \pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{/tmp/tex2pdf.-4ebbea1a09952fd5/figures/paper-figure-3.png}}
  \caption{Two independent sources of measurement mismatch. (A) The same
  illustrated vegetation, soil, and shadow are integrated over different
  pixel footprints; grids are schematic and not to scale. (B) Read from
  top to bottom: the upper curve is the input reflectance spectrum; the
  lower curves show how strongly each target band weights each wavelength.
  They are response functions, not output spectra. Applying a response
  curve to the input produces one weighted reflectance value, shown
  schematically by the matching colored output label and defined in
  Equation 1. The Gaussian profiles use packaged Landsat 8 OLI and
  MicaSense centers and full widths at half maximum (FWHM), not official
  measured response curves (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026k}{Earth Lab,
  2026k}); the spectrum is illustrative.}\label{fig:paper1}
  \end{figure}
  
  \section{State of the field}\label{state-of-the-field}
  
  HyTools is SpectralBridge’s closest methodological relative and part of
  its NEON lineage. HLS and Sen2Like harmonize satellite products; FORCE
  processes satellite archives; Snakemake and Nextflow orchestrate general
  pipelines (\citeproc{ref-paper_chlus2025}{Chlus et al., 2025};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_claverie2018}{Claverie et al., 2018};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_ditommaso2017}{Di Tommaso et al., 2017};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_frantz2019}{Frantz, 2019};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_ju2025}{Ju et al., 2025};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_koster2012}{Köster \& Rahmann, 2012};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_saunier2022}{Saunier et al., 2022}). In the systems
  compared here, none combines the separate NEON and drone routes with
  collection-level artifact and QA contracts
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026x}{Earth Lab, 2026y},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026j}{2026j}). SpectralBridge’s
  contribution is this sensor-aware integration, restart checks, and
  grouped summaries - not a new correction method or harmonization
  product.
  
  \section{Software design}\label{software-design}
  
  The workflow addresses geometry normalization and target-band weighting,
  while preserving geospatial products for downstream spatial
  reconciliation. It does not create a common spatial support; nominally
  matching bands may also require bandpass adjustment
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_chander2013}{Chander et al., 2013};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026z}{Earth Lab, 2026aa},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{2026m};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_roy2016}{Roy et al., 2016}). NEON provides the dense
  spectrum from which configured MicaSense- and Landsat-like products are
  constructed. Independently acquired drone data follow a separate
  preparation path (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026z}{Earth Lab, 2026aa},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026h}{2026h};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_nationalecologicalobservatorynetwork2026}{National
  Ecological Observatory Network, 2026}). Thus, Drone → NEON → Landsat
  describes a comparison design, not a physical conversion chain. A named
  target product reflects configured weighting and any recorded
  adjustment, while retaining NEON’s original spatial support.
  
  \subsection{Geometry and terrain}\label{geometry-and-terrain}
  
  For NEON, the workflow estimates bandwise SCS+C topographic terms, then
  applies RossThick and LiDenseR BRDF terms using available solar and view
  geometry (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026r}{Earth Lab, 2026r},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026ab}{2026ac};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_queally2022}{Queally et al., 2022}). These are
  established methods, with code lineage to HyTools acknowledged in the
  implementation (\citeproc{ref-paper_chlus2025}{Chlus et al., 2025};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_li1992}{Li \& Strahler, 1992};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_ross1981}{Ross, 1981};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_roujean1992}{Roujean et al., 1992};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_schaaf2002}{Schaaf et al., 2002};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_soenen2005}{Soenen et al., 2005};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_wanner1995}{Wanner et al., 1995}). Invalid geometry
  ratios receive neutral factors, which QA should report. Both corrections
  share one output cube, so their separate effects cannot be isolated from
  that artifact.
  
  \subsection{Spectral response}\label{spectral-response}
  
  Different instruments weight different parts of the spectrum, so
  matching band names do not guarantee matching measurements
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_chander2013}{Chander et al., 2013};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_roy2016}{Roy et al., 2016}). Given source
  reflectance samples \(\rho_i\) and target-band weights \(s_{b,i}\)
  sampled on the same source-band grid, SpectralBridge computes
  
  \[
  \hat{\rho}_b = \frac{\sum_i \rho_i s_{b,i}}{\sum_i s_{b,i}}. \tag{1}
  \]
  
  The implementation normalizes supplied response weights by their
  discrete sum; it does not perform wavelength-interval quadrature.
  Weights must therefore match the source grid and intended spectral
  support. Actual Landsat weighting requires measured relative spectral
  responses, not band centers and widths alone
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_barsi2014}{Barsi et al., 2014}). A deterministic
  fixture checks this arithmetic to an absolute tolerance of
  \(2 \times 10^{-7}\) (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026s}{Earth Lab,
  2026s}, \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026ae}{2026af}). Source atmospheric
  correction is inherited from NEON; some configured Landsat-like products
  also receive a separate empirical brightness adjustment
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026l}{Earth Lab, 2026l};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_nationalecologicalobservatorynetwork2026}{National
  Ecological Observatory Network, 2026}). These configured representations
  are not proof of physical agreement with another sensor
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_claverie2023}{Claverie, 2023};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_ju2025}{Ju et al., 2025}).
  
  \subsection{Spatial support}\label{spatial-support}
  
  Matching band spaces does not make a crown-scale spectrum and a Landsat
  pixel the same spatial mixture (\citeproc{ref-paper_huang2002}{Huang et
  al., 2002}; \citeproc{ref-paper_teillet1997}{Teillet, 1997};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_woodcock1987}{Woodcock \& Strahler, 1987}).
  SpectralBridge preserves georeferenced rasters and polygon extractions
  but leaves grid alignment, overlap, and aggregation to the user, because
  these choices determine which observations contribute
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_dungan2002}{Dungan et al., 2002};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{Earth Lab, 2026m},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026h}{2026h}).
  
  \subsection{Architecture and data
  flow}\label{architecture-and-data-flow}
  
  \autoref{fig:paper2} aligns the two observation routes and the shared
  collection-analysis path. NEON and drone processing share most stages;
  their main differences are at the edges: drone processing adds
  local-image staging and may use an affine mapping, while NEON’s dense
  spectrum supports target-band convolution. Each route writes named
  artifacts for distinct measurement states, linking the source to
  corrected and target-band products
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026e}{Earth Lab, 2026e},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{2026m}).
  
  \begin{figure}
  \centering
  \pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{/tmp/tex2pdf.-4ebbea1a09952fd5/figures/paper-figure-6.png}}
  \caption{The NEON and drone routes share most stages and retain similar
  rasters, tables, and QA/provenance. Drone processing adds local input
  staging and may apply a configured affine mapping; NEON processing
  convolves its dense spectrum with target-band responses. The shared
  collection path streams selected, aligned products into grouped fits and
  diagnostics. The diagram shows intended workflow structure, not one
  campaign’s results (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026j}{Earth Lab,
  2026j}, \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{2026m},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026h}{2026h},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026x}{2026y},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026a}{2026a}).}\label{fig:paper2}
  \end{figure}
  
  The architecture evolved from a HyTools-based workflow; HyTools is no
  longer a runtime dependency, but lineage remains in NEON access, ENVI
  writing, and correction components (\citeproc{ref-paper_chlus2025}{Chlus
  et al., 2025}; \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2025a}{Earth Lab, 2025a},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2025b}{2025b},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026i}{2026i},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026t}{2026u},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026r}{2026r}). The NEON route applies
  configured geometry corrections and spectral weighting to its source
  cube. The drone route retains native bands and may apply reviewed affine
  coefficients, without runtime coefficient fitting or spectral
  convolution (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026e}{Earth Lab, 2026e},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{2026m},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026h}{2026h},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026c}{2026c}). Collection analysis streams
  aligned ENVI windows into pooled, flightline-, and site-level fits. It
  accumulates compact statistics but still reads source rasters; no
  performance advantage is claimed without a matched benchmark
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026a}{Earth Lab, 2026a},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026aa}{2026ab},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026b}{2026b}). Named files serve as stage
  interfaces, not disposable cache. This design lets users inspect
  intermediate states, check stage-specific QA, and reuse valid products
  after interruption; it costs storage and I/O, and does not serialize
  every realized parameter. Restart therefore supports recovery, not
  guaranteed exact replay. Completion is request-specific: every
  configured output must exist and be readable
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026j}{Earth Lab, 2026j},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026d}{2026d},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026ad}{2026ae},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026u}{2026v}).
  
  \begin{figure}
  \centering
  \pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{/tmp/tex2pdf.-4ebbea1a09952fd5/figures/paper-figure-7.png}}
  \caption{Four questions govern a multi-stage scientific workflow. Normal
  process termination does not establish production of the requested
  scientific state; artifact existence does not establish QA; and QA does
  not establish agreement with independent observations. Remote-sensing
  validation frameworks likewise treat validation as staged evidence
  rather than a binary declaration
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_morisette2006}{Morisette et al., 2006};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_origo2020}{Origo et al., 2020}). The examples in the
  Research impact statement illustrate these separate checks
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026p}{Earth Lab, 2026p};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_spectralbridgeprojectteam2026}{SpectralBridge
  project team, 2026}).}\label{fig:paper3}
  \end{figure}
  
  The package is OS-independent, requires Python 3.10 or newer, and is
  distributed under the GNU General Public License version 3 (GPLv3);
  installation and usage are documented in its README
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026v}{Earth Lab, 2026w},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026x}{2026y}). The evidence spans version
  2.2.0 at f7e7ddd for the historical NEON route and c86da6a for later
  bulk work and 2.3.0rc1 preparation, but supplied run records do not link
  every result to an exact revision
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026y}{Earth Lab, 2026z},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026g}{2026g}). The public release page
  listed v1.0.0 at commit 9d1de28 as latest on 12 October 2026; the cited
  Zenodo DOI is a pre-rename archive, not a record for these results
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026w}{Earth Lab, 2026x},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026f}{2026f}). The supplementary material
  distinguishes these historical source and execution records.
  
  \subsection{Reuse potential}\label{reuse-potential}
  
  Reuse is most direct for NEON products and drone observations that match
  the supported input schemas. Users can configure target-band responses
  or reviewed affine registries, while preserving exact wavelength
  definitions and coefficient provenance; adding spatial aggregation or a
  new sensor remains a study-specific extension. The ENVI, Parquet, and
  JSON products can then be inspected or consumed by downstream analysis
  tools (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026m}{Earth Lab, 2026m},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026h}{2026h},
  \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026x}{2026y}).
  
  \section{Research impact statement}\label{research-impact-statement}
  
  The examples below come from the authors’ ongoing research workflow for
  comparing NEON, drone, and Landsat reflectance; they are developer-team
  applications, not evidence of external adoption. Deterministic fixtures
  cover convolution, correction, extraction, QA, restart, and
  collection-analysis contracts (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026ac}{Earth
  Lab, 2026ad}, \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026n}{2026n};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_kanewala2014}{Kanewala \& Bieman, 2014}). In the
  R10C NEON example, the declared route produced 18 readable Parquet
  outputs, while QA retained warnings, including 68 poor-quality
  wavelength bands that were reported but not masked. This demonstrates
  artifact completion and warning preservation, not correction efficacy or
  agreement with Landsat (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026p}{Earth Lab,
  2026p}, \citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026q}{2026q}). A separate 43-flight
  drone attempt exposes the value of explicit completion checks:
  orchestration labeled all flights processed, but required ENVI and
  Parquet outputs were absent
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_spectralbridgeprojectteam2026}{SpectralBridge
  project team, 2026}). A supplied bulk analysis summarizes 122
  flightlines, but its executed software revision and run manifest were
  not provided (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab2026o}{Earth Lab, 2026o};
  \citeproc{ref-paper_spectralbridgebulkevidencebundle2026}{SpectralBridge
  bulk evidence bundle, 2026}). Together, these cases show how the
  workflow can expose incomplete production, preserve warnings, and
  summarize collections while making the limits of the evidence visible.
  They do not establish independent cross-sensor accuracy, a performance
  advantage, improved ecological inference, or adoption beyond the
  developer team.
  
  \section{AI usage disclosure}\label{ai-usage-disclosure}
  
  The authors report beginning with GPT-3.5 for early consultation and
  subsequently using all later models available in their workflow. This
  early history predates the repository’s partial prompt log, which
  records OpenAI Codex and Cursor Agent. Recorded model labels for Codex
  include GPT-5, GPT-5 family (exact deployment identifier not exposed),
  and GPT-6. Cursor entries and most historical Codex entries do not
  identify a model; unrecorded model versions are not inferred
  (\citeproc{ref-paper_earthlab_ai_transparency2026}{Earth Lab, 2026t}).
  
  Logged requests cover software implementation and refactoring,
  regression and contract tests, pipeline debugging, source and licensing
  audits, documentation and release preparation, QA visualization,
  analysis-table checks, and manuscript conversion and citation checks.
  Requested verification includes automated tests, linting, documentation
  checks, installed-artifact smoke tests, and inspection of numerical and
  rendered outputs. The log begins on 21 March 2026, after development was
  underway, and records user requests rather than accepted outputs or
  proof that every requested check was completed.
  
  Human team members reviewed proposed software changes, citations, and
  calculations. AI output was not treated as scientific evidence; the
  authors retain responsibility for the software, scientific
  interpretation, and manuscript. {[}AUTHOR INPUT REQUIRED: confirm that
  human authors reviewed, edited and validated all AI-assisted outputs and
  made the core design decisions.{]}
  
